This is a classic "Heisenbug" in Material UI. Because you have a DialogActions container with a p: 3 padding and a borderTop, the browser is technically considering the **entire footer** as part of the click surface.
When you click the "Close" icon (the IconButton at the top right) or the "Cross" on the backdrop, the browser performs the onClick event, but the **Focus Trapper** is still alive during that 300ms fade-out transition. It sees that Button (Download Batch) and—because it's the first focusable element inside—it snaps the focus to it.
To finally kill this, we need to remove the button from the **Tab order** and **Programmatically force focus away** during the closing sequence.
### The "Surgical" Fix
#### 1. Add tabIndex={-1} to the Button
By setting tabIndex={-1}, we tell the browser: "This button is not allowed to be focused by tabbing or programmatic focus traps." This is the strongest way to stop a focus-thief.
Find your **Download Batch** button (inside the vidDetails conditional block) and update it:
```jsx
<Button
  variant="outlined"
  size="small"
  startIcon={<FileDownloadIcon />}
  onClick={handleDownloadExcel}
  disabled={!viewingBatchId}
  tabIndex={-1} // <--- ADD THIS
  onMouseDown={(e) => e.preventDefault()} // <--- ADD THIS: Prevents focus on click
  sx={{
    borderColor: "#217346",
    color: "#217346",
    "&:hover": { backgroundColor: "#e8f5e9", borderColor: "#1e6b40" },
    // Completely nuke all focus states
    "&:focus, &.Mui-focusVisible, &:active": {
      outline: "none !important",
      boxShadow: "none !important",
      backgroundColor: "transparent !important",
      border: "1px solid #217346 !important"
    }
  }}
>
  Download Batch
</Button>

```
#### 2. The Global "Blur" Strategy
Since you mentioned clicking the "Cross" (Close Icon) also triggers it, that IconButton is *also* grabbing focus. Let's make handleCloseViewModal the ultimate focus-killer:
```javascript
  const handleCloseViewModal = () => {
    // 1. Force blur on whatever is currently focused (Close Icon or the Dialog itself)
    if (document.activeElement instanceof HTMLElement) {
      document.activeElement.blur();
    }
    
    // 2. Set focus to the body so it doesn't try to find a button inside the closing modal
    document.body.focus();

    setTimeout(() => {
      setIsViewModalOpen(false);
      setViewingBatchId(null);
      setIsDescExpanded(false);
    }, 300);
  };

```
### Why this is the final solution:
 * **e.preventDefault() on onMouseDown**: This is a pro-tip. By preventing the default browser behavior on mouse-down, you stop the button from ever transitioning into the :active or :focus state in the first place when you click it.
 * **tabIndex={-1}**: This removes the button from the internal document.tabOrder. If the Dialog's Focus Trap tries to "find" the next focusable item, it will effectively skip over your button entirely.
 * **document.body.focus()**: This is the "nuclear" reset. By moving the focus to the <body> tag, you rip the focus away from any component inside the fading modal.
Apply these, and the button will become "un-selectable" by the Focus Trap mechanism. It will remain perfectly clickable, but it will lose its ability to "catch" the highlight when the modal closes.
